From d8a7ea47928ea06ca49dc9feb55edf7eaeb6077f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tacitech <contact@tacitek.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 19:03:25 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] AP_Math: make Vector2 may_alias so Vector3::xy() is
 defined

Vector3<T>::xy() returns the x and y of a Vector3 as a Vector2 by casting
the Vector3 pointer. That breaks the strict-aliasing rule: GCC may assume
an access through the Vector2 does not touch the Vector3 and reorder them.

GCC 10.2 for armhf at -O3 (SITL on a Zynq ARM board) did exactly that in
AC_Loiter::set_pilot_desired_acceleration_rad():

    _predicted_accel_ne_mss = predicted_accel_ned_mss.xy();

the 8-byte copy of the Vector2 view was scheduled before the Vector3
returned by lean_angles_rad_to_accel_NED_mss() had been stored to the
stack, so it copied stale stack contents. LOITER ignored the roll and
pitch sticks, and POSHOLD (which calls clear_pilot_desired_acceleration()
every loop) drifted on garbage depending on what was left on the stack.

ChibiOS boards build with -fno-strict-aliasing and never see this. With
__may_alias__ on Vector2 the Vector2 view may alias anything, and the
store and the copy stay in order (checked in the assembly).
---
 libraries/AP_Math/vector2.h | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/libraries/AP_Math/vector2.h b/libraries/AP_Math/vector2.h
index 4ac6198..a2539f0 100644
--- a/libraries/AP_Math/vector2.h
+++ b/libraries/AP_Math/vector2.h
@@ -41,8 +41,13 @@
 #include <AP_Common/AP_Common.h>
 #include "ftype.h"
 
+// may_alias: Vector3<T>::xy() hands out the x and y of a Vector3 as a Vector2 (a cast of the
+// Vector3 pointer). Without it that is a strict-aliasing violation and GCC may move the read of
+// the Vector2 view ahead of the stores to the Vector3 (seen with GCC 10 armhf -O3 in
+// AC_Loiter::set_pilot_desired_acceleration_rad(): the predicted acceleration was read before the
+// Vector3 it comes from had been written).
 template <typename T>
-struct Vector2
+struct __attribute__((__may_alias__)) Vector2
 {
     T x, y;
 
